  "text" : "Today, we'll be preparing uh 6 types of shows with 2 micrometer beat samples for imaging tomorrow. What we're gonna be doing is describing the methods. So 1st I will be making a one to 100 stock solution using 2 micrometer now red beads. And then I'll be pipetting those on top of a cover slip, which is present in each 6 wells of the 6 well plate. And on top of the cover slip will be a silicone ring in which inside the ring, the beat sample stock solution will be pipe added into. Then we'll be preparing the activated gel solution. And this works by taking a solution A and solution G from the fridge, by pinning 500 microliters of solution G to an empty 2 milliliter, um, Eppendorf tube, then pipe ending 600 microliters of solution A into the provided activation tube and vortexing it to make the activation buffer. Then we pipe it 25 microliters of the activation buffer to the 500 microliters of solution G to make the activated geling solution. Now, this gelling solution is not stable and should be used within 15 minutes before, um, premature gelling happens. Um, and therefore, uh, once that once that's prepared, we will take the activated gel solution and pour it into the, each of the 6 silicone rings inside the 6 wall plate. And then to ensure that the activated gelling solution soaks into the beat sample and the beat sample is fully cast into the activated gelling solution, will be putting it in 15 minutes in 4 degrees Celsius. Uh, and then from there, we're going to be doing the geling process, which involves putting it in 37 degrees Celsius for 2 hours in an incubator, uh, with a Kim white present in each of the wells of the 6 wall plate. And then after that, taking it out and putting it in the fridge at 4 degrees Celsius, uh, ready for detachment tomorrow and for homogenization and then imaging."
